{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**Di tonic chords in the key of G major**\r\n\r\n| Scale degree | Chord (quality) | Common open / barre shape |\r\n|--------------|----------------|---------------------------|\r\n| I            | **G** maj      | 320033 |\r\n| ii           | **Am** min      | x02210 |\r\n| iii          | **Bm** min      | x24432 |\r\n| IV           | **C** maj       | x32010 |\r\n| V            | **D** maj       | xx0232 |\r\n| vi           | **Em** min      | 022000 |\r\n| vii°         | **F♯dim** dim  | X24242 (often voiced as an F♯dim7) |\r\n\r\nBelow are the exact fret‑positions for the open‑chord shapes (vexTab blocks can be copied straight into a VexTab‑compatible editor).\r\n\r\n**G maj** – 320033  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/6.2/5.0/4.0/3.0/2.3/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Am** – x02210  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/5.2/4.2/3.1/2.0/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Bm** – x24432 (barre at 2nd fret)  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/6.4/5.4/4.3/3.2/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**C maj** – x32010  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/5.2/4.0/3.1/2.0/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**D maj** – xx0232  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/4.2/3.3/2.2/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Em** – 022000  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/6.2/5.2/4.0/3.0/2.0/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**F♯dim** – X24242 (often played as an F♯dim7)  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (4/5.2/4.2/3.4/2.2/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThese seven chords form the harmonic backbone of any progression in G major. Use the V‑chord (D) to create a strong resolution back to the I‑chord (G), and feel free to add sevenths (e.g., G maj7, Am7, Bm7, C maj7, D7, Em7) for richer voicings. Happy strumming!","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":6882,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":7,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-37","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"what chords are in the key of G","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":7}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"When you move from a **D major** triad (D‑F#‑A) to a **B minor** triad (B‑D‑F#) the smoothest voice‑leading is to keep any note that both chords share.\r\n\r\n**Common tones:**  \r\n- **D** – the root of the D chord and the minor‑third of Bm.  \r\n- **F#** – the major‑third of D and the fifth of Bm.\r\n\r\nSo you can let **either D or F# keep ringing** while the other voices move (A → B, or D → B, etc.). In practice guitarists often let the **D** stay because it’s the open 4th string (or the 12th‑fret D) and it lands on a strong chord tone of Bm.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example voicings (open‑string friendly)\r\n\r\n**D major (open)** – strings 6‑1: x‑x‑0‑2‑3‑2  \r\n**B minor (open shape)** – strings 6‑1: x‑2‑4‑4‑3‑2  \r\n\r\nHere the **D** on the 4th string (open) rings through both chords.\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :h (0/4) (2/2) (3/1) (2/2) (2/3) (4/2)\r\n```\r\n\r\n*(First chord: D major; second chord: B minor – the D on the 4th string is held.)*\r\n\r\nIf you prefer to hold **F#** instead, use a barre shape that keeps the high‑E string at the 2nd fret (F#) for both chords.\r\n\r\nBoth options give a clean, connected transition; choose the one that fits the voicing and timbre you want in the context of the song.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2349,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":3,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-40","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"going from D to Bm, which note can i leave ringing","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":2}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The only pitch that belongs to both an A‑major chord (A C♯ E) and a D‑major chord (D F♯ A) is **A**.  \r\n\r\nUse the open A string (5th string, 0 fret) or the 2nd‑fret on the G string for a convenient drone that sits under both harmony. If you want a richer texture you can double the A an octave higher on the high‑E string (open E → 2nd fret E = A).","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":1779,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":3,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":1,"id":"f-48","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"writing a drone part under two chords, what notes are in both A and D","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":2}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**Transposed chords (down 2 semitones)**  \r\n\r\n| Original | ↓2 ½‑steps | Guitar shape (no capo) |\r\n|----------|------------|------------------------|\r\n| **Bb**   | **Ab**     | 4‑barre (root on 6th string) |\r\n| **Gm**   | **Fm**     | 1‑barre (root on 6th string) |\r\n| **Eb**   | **Db**     | x‑4‑6‑6‑5‑4 (or 4‑barre) |\r\n| **F**    | **Eb**     | x‑6‑8‑8‑8‑6 (or 6‑barre) |\r\n\r\n### Playable voicings\r\n\r\n**Ab (major)** – 4‑barre, root on 6‑E  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (4/6.4/5.6/4.5.4/4.4/3.4/2.4/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fm (minor)** – 1‑barre, root on 6‑E  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (1/6.1/5.1/4.1/3.1/2.1/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Db (major)** – x‑4‑6‑6‑5‑4 (no full barre)  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (4/5.4/4.6.6/3.5.4/2)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Eb (major)** – x‑6‑8‑8‑8‑6 (full barre on 6th fret)  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (6/6.6/5.8/4.8/3.8/2.6/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Quick tips\r\n- **Strum pattern**: Down‑down‑up‑up‑down‑up works well for ballads in this key.  \r\n- **Barre comfort**: If barre chords feel tough, use the partial‑barre shapes above for Db and Eb.  \r\n- **Capo alternative**: Keep the original shapes (Bb, Gm, Eb, F) and place a capo on the **2nd fret**; the sounding pitch will be a whole step lower (Ab, Fm, Db, Eb).  \r\n\r\nLet me know if you need a specific voicing for a different register or a tab for the whole progression!","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2424,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":3,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-93","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"singer wants it 2 half steps lower: Bb Gm Eb F","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":2}
{"agent":"theory","answer":"In E natural minor the diatonic triads are:\r\n\r\n- **i – Em** (E‑G‑B) – minor\r\n- **ii° – F♯dim** (F♯‑A‑C) – diminished\r\n- **III – G major** (G‑B‑D) – major\r\n- **iv – Am** (A‑C‑E) – minor\r\n- **v – Bm** (B‑D‑F♯) – minor (in E harmonic minor this becomes **V – B major**: B‑D♯‑F♯)\r\n- **VI – C major** (C‑E‑G) – major\r\n- **VII – D major** (D‑F♯‑A) – subtonic major (in E harmonic minor this becomes **vii° – D♯dim**: D♯‑F♯‑A)\r\n\r\nThus the natural‑minor set uses a subtonic major on the seventh degree, while the harmonic‑minor alteration raises the seventh to D♯, giving a leading‑tone diminished triad (vii°) and a major V chord.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":25867,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":16,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-96","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"list the 7 chords in e minor pls","routing":"llm","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":16}
